{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9f2ba5463ac35b98", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t MiDashengLM-Gen: Unified Audio Scene Generation via LLM-Driven Autoregressive Flow Matching Report GitHub Issue × \n2\tTitle: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub arXiv is \n3\tnow an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Abstract \n4\t1 Introduction 2 Related Work 2.1 Single-Type Audio Generation Text-to-Audio (TTA). Text-to-Music (TTM). Text-to-Speech \n5\t(TTS). 2.2 Mixed-Type Audio Generation Task-Specific Multi-Type Generation. Unified Audio Scene Generation. 3 Method \n6\t3.1 Autoregressive LLM Backbone Structured Multi-View Audio Scene Captioning Audio-Text Alignment Autoregressive LLM \n7\tInput Formulation 3.2 Per-Token Conditional Flow Matching Token-Level Generation. Training Objective. Inference. 3.3 \n8\tImplementation Details Model Configuration. Training and Inference. 4 Experiments 4.1 Experimental Setup 4.2 Sound \n9\tEffect and Music Generation 4.3 Speech Generation Speech Generation on Seed-TTS. Multilingual Speech Generation. \n10\tEmotion Expressiveness. 4.4 MECAT Benchmark 4.5 Ablation Experiments 5 Discussion and Conclusion 6 Acknowledgement \n11\tReferences License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2608.11804v1 [eess.AS] 12 Aug 2026 Mi DashengLM-Gen: Unified Audio Scene Generation \n12\tvia LLM-Driven Autoregressive Flow Matching Xingwei Sun Affiliation: MiLM Plus, Xiaomi Inc., Beijing, China Heinrich \n13\tDinkel Affiliation: MiLM Plus, Xiaomi Inc., Beijing, China Gang Li Affiliation: MiLM Plus, Xiaomi Inc., Beijing, China \n14\tJiahao Mei Affiliation: MiLM Plus, Xiaomi Inc., Beijing, China Affiliation: X-LANCE Lab, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, \n15\tShanghai, China Yadong Niu Affiliation: MiLM Plus, Xiaomi Inc., Beijing, China Zerui Han Affiliation: MiLM Plus, Xiaomi \n16\tInc., Beijing, China Yuepeng Jiang Affiliation: MiLM Plus, Xiaomi Inc., Beijing, China Jiahao Zhou Affiliation: MiLM \n17\tPlus, Xiaomi Inc., Beijing, China Lichun Fan Affiliation: MiLM Plus, Xiaomi Inc., Beijing, China Jian Luan Affiliation: \n18\tMiLM Plus, Xiaomi Inc., Beijing, China Abstract Generating coherent audio scenes that simultaneously blend speech, \n19\tmusic, and sound effects remains a significant challenge. Current approaches typically rely on a disjointed pipeline \n20\twhere a frozen, decoupled text encoder feeds a separate audio decoder, limiting cross-modal optimization and leading to \n21\tpoor speech intelligibility. To overcome these limitations, we introduce MiDashengLM-Gen, an end-to-end framework that \n22\tcouples a pre-trained Large Language Model (LLM) with per-token conditional flow matching for autoregressive, \n23\tvariable-length mixed-audio scene generation. MiDashengLM-Gen represents a first approach for general text-to-audio \n24\tgeneration with one end-to-end trained model. Empirical evaluations demonstrate that MiDashengLM-Gen drastically \n25\timproves speech intelligibility over existing unified models. On the Seed-TTS benchmark, English Word Error Rate (WER) \n26\tdrops from 12.15% to 2.79%, approaching the performance of dedicated Text-to-Speech (TTS) systems (1.24%). Furthermore, \n27\tthe framework extends effectively to multilingual settings, yielding highly competitive multilingual WERs compared to \n28\texisting baselines. Lastly, the model maintains competitive mixed-audio generation quality on the MECAT benchmark. Code \n29\tand checkpoints are available at 1 1 1 https://github.com/xiaomi-research/midashenglm-gen and 2 2 2 \n30\thttps://huggingface.co/mispeech/midashenglm-gen , and the demo page is available at 3 3 3 \n31\thttps://xingws.github.io/midashenglm-gen-demo/ . 1 Introduction Generating coherent audio scenes that naturally blend \n32\tintelligible speech, music, and environmental sounds is essential for applications such as film production, game \n33\tdesign, and immersive media. While single-type generation models have made remarkable progress in Text-to-Speech (TTS) \n34\t14 ; 2 , Text-to-Music (TTM) 5 ; 1 , and Text-to-Audio (TTA) 24 ; 16 , they operate within isolated domains and cannot \n35\tproduce realistic mixed-audio scenes where multiple sound sources coexist and interact. In contrast, task-unified \n36\tmodels 41 ; 39 consolidate multiple generation tasks under a single architecture, but produce each audio type \n37\tindependently without modeling the temporal coordination, energy balance, and acoustic consistency required for \n38\tcoherent scenes where speech, music, and sound effects overlap. Dasheng AudioGen 28 made a first step toward this goal \n39\tby introducing structured multi-view captions for fine-grained scene generation, decomposing complex acoustic scenes \n40\tinto textual descriptions and demonstrating that high-dimensional semantic-acoustic latent representations enable \n41\teffective unified generation. However, because Dasheng AudioGen’s architecture relies on a frozen text encoder and a \n42\tnon-autoregressive diffusion transformer, the model is fundamentally limited to fixed-length, monolingual outputs with \n43\tsuboptimal speech intelligibility. These limitations stem from fundamental architectural choices rather than the \n44\tstructured caption or latent space design, motivating an end-to-end generation backbone that jointly optimizes text \n45\tunderstanding and audio synthesis. To address these limitations, we propose MiDashengLM-Gen, which retains the \n46\tstructured multi-view captions and the DashengTokenizer latent space from Dasheng AudioGen but replaces the generation \n47\tbackbone with a pre-trained large language model (LLM) combined with per-token conditional flow matching (Figure 1 ). \n48\tThe LLM jointly encodes text and audio, eliminating the separate frozen text encoder and thereby enabling multilingual \n49\tgeneration and improved data utilization. This architecture brings speech intelligibility close to dedicated TTS \n50\tsystems while maintaining mixed-audio scene generation capability. MiDashengLM-Gen combines LLM sequence modeling with \n51\tper-token flow matching to enable autoregressive, variable-length, coherent mixed-audio scene generation within a \n52\tunified framework. Table 1 compares the capabilities and architectural features of previous approaches. Table 1 : \n53\tGeneration capabilities of representative audio generation models. Var.-Len. indicates whether the model supports \n54\tvariable-length generation. Model Var.-Len. SFX Music Speech Scene Multiling. TangoFlux 16 × \\times ✓ ✓ × \\times \n55\t× \\times - MusicGen 5 ✓ × \\times ✓ × \\times × \\times - Qwen3-TTS 14 ✓ × \\times × \\times ✓ × \\times ✓ \n56\tUniFlow-Audio 39 × \\times ✓ ✓ ✓ × \\times × \\times Dasheng AudioGen 28 × \\times ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ × \\times \n57\tMiDashengLM-Gen ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ Our contributions are: 1. Autoregressive generation with per-token flow \n58\tmatching. We introduce MiDashengLM-Gen, a framework that integrates a pre-trained LLM backbone with per-token \n59\tconditional flow matching for unified audio scene generation, supporting variable-length output via a learned stop \n60\thead. 2. LLM-conditioned flow matching with high-dimensional audio latents. We demonstrate the viability of utilizing \n61\thigh-dimensional semantic-acoustic latents for LLM-conditioned per-token flow matching. Furthermore, we identify a \n62\tcritical convergence prerequisite: the DiT decoder width must strictly exceed the audio latent dimensionality—a \n63\tstructural property that generalizes across LLM scales and latent dimensions. 3. Audio-text alignment pre-training for \n64\tcross-modal generation. We integrate an audio-text alignment stage that maps audio latents into the LLM’s token space \n65\tprior to generation training. Ablation studies confirm that this alignment phase is strictly required for viable \n66\tcross-modal synthesis. 4. Strong speech intelligibility with competitive mixed-audio performance. MiDashengLM-Gen \n67\tachieves speech intelligibility approaching dedicated TTS systems, maintains competitive mixed-audio scene generation \n68\tacross multiple benchmarks, and supports multilingual generation. 2 Related Work 2.1 Single-Type Audio Generation \n69\tText-to-Audio (TTA). Generating sound effects from text descriptions has been primarily addressed through diffusion 13 \n70\tand flow-matching 23 frameworks. AudioLDM 24 introduced CLAP-conditioned latent diffusion over compressed audio \n71\trepresentations, and Make-An-Audio 15 extended this paradigm with temporal structure modeling and prompt augmentation \n72\tstrategies. TangoFlux 16 replaced iterative diffusion with single-step flow matching combined with preference-based \n73\ttraining, significantly reducing inference cost while maintaining audio fidelity. All these approaches target isolated \n74\tenvironmental sounds and do not address speech or music generation. Text-to-Music (TTM). Music generation presents \n75\tunique challenges in long-term structural harmony, typically addressed via discrete-token or continuous-latent \n76\tarchitectures. MusicLM 1 pioneered a hierarchical approach that first generates semantic tokens and then refines them \n77\tinto acoustic tokens. MusicGen 5 simplified this pipeline by directly predicting EnCodec tokens with a single \n78\ttext-conditioned transformer. On the continuous side, AudioLDM2 25 extends latent diffusion to a shared audio-language \n79\tembedding space, and JEN-1 22 applies flow matching for controllable music creation. Despite strong musical output, \n80\tnone of these systems can produce intelligible speech or model interactions between music and other concurrent sounds. \n81\tText-to-Speech (TTS). The TTS field has shifted from pipeline-based synthesis such as Tacotron 33 and FastSpeech 32 to \n82\tfully end-to-end architectures like VITS 20 that jointly model linguistic and acoustic features. Discrete-token \n83\tlanguage models including VALL-E 38 and SoundStorm 3 then demonstrated that speech can be effectively cast as a \n84\tsequence prediction task over audio codecs, opening the path for LLM-based TTS. Building on this paradigm, large-scale \n85\tsystems such as Seed-TTS 2 , Qwen3-TTS 14 , and MiniMax-Speech 43 achieve near-human quality with zero-shot voice \n86\tcloning and multilingual support. Most recently, flow-matching approaches including F5-TTS 4 and CosyVoice 3 9 offer \n87\tefficient non-autoregressive synthesis with fine-grained prosody and emotion control. However, all TTS systems produce \n88\tspeech in isolation, without accounting for background music, environmental acoustics, or other simultaneously \n89\toccurring audio events. 2.2 Mixed-Type Audio Generation Task-Specific Multi-Type Generation. Several architectures \n90\tsupport generating different audio types but treat each as a separate task. UniAudio 41 first unified multiple audio \n91\ttasks by encoding all types as flat token sequences processed by a shared language model. AudioX 37 broadened the input \n92\tspace by conditioning a diffusion transformer on heterogeneous modalities beyond text. UniFlow-Audio 39 further adopted \n93\ta common flow-matching backbone with modality-specific adapters for flexible generation. Although these models \n94\tconsolidate multiple capabilities, they generate each audio type independently and cannot produce scenes where speech, \n95\tmusic, and environmental sounds overlap and interact coherently. Unified Audio Scene Generation. BagPiper 36 \n96\tdemonstrates mixed audio generation via an autoregressive framework, but relies on very long unstructured captions that \n97\tmake fine-grained control difficult. Further, Dasheng AudioGen 28 proposed structured multi-view captions to decompose \n98\tscenes into distinct textual descriptions (global caption, transcript, music, sound events, and environment), and \n99\tshowed that generating in a high-dimensional semantic-acoustic latent space is more effective than using compressed VAE \n100\tfeatures. However, its non-autoregressive architecture restricts output to a fixed duration and relies on a separate \n101\tfrozen text encoder, decoupling text semantics from audio synthesis. In summary, existing models either generate audio \n102\ttypes independently (UniAudio, UniFlow-Audio) or are constrained by frozen text encoders and fixed output durations \n103\t(Dasheng AudioGen). To address these constraints, we integrate an LLM backbone that jointly optimizes text \n104\tunderstanding and audio generation with per-token flow matching, allowing for flexible output lengths and coherent \n105\tscene generation. 3 Method Our goal is to generate coherent audio scenes from text descriptions, where the scene may \n106\tsimultaneously contain speech, music, sound effects, and environmental acoustics. We formulate this as autoregressive \n107\tconditional generation: the model produces audio tokens sequentially, with each token conditioned on the text \n108\tdescription and all previously generated audio latents. Our framework combines autoregressive LLM sequence modeling \n109\twith per-token continuous flow matching. A DiT-based flow matching decoder generates continuous audio tokens, \n110\tconditioned on the LLM hidden state at each autoregressive step. We adopt the encoder of a custom MiDashengLM-0.6B \n111\tmodel 4 4 4 https://huggingface.co/mispeech/midashenglm-0.6b-fp32 as our unified semantic-acoustic audio tokenizer, \n112\tfollowing the DashengTokenizer 8 architecture. Structured multi-view captions provide fine-grained supervision across \n113\tsix textual views. Figures 1(a) and 1(b) shows the overall model architecture. (a) Training pipeline with flow matching \n114\tloss. (b) Inference pipeline with autoregressive generation. Figure 1 : Training and inference pipelines. 3.1 \n115\tAutoregressive LLM Backbone We employ a pre-trained causal language model (Qwen3 40 ) as the sequential backbone for \n116\taudio scene generation. Unlike prior unified models that require separate text encoders (e.g., T5) and cross-attention \n117\tmechanisms, our approach uses the LLM’s pre-trained representations to jointly encode text and audio within a single \n118\tunified model. This eliminates architectural complexity while leveraging the LLM’s strong language understanding for \n119\ttext-to-audio alignment. Structured Multi-View Audio Scene Captioning To provide fine-grained supervision for complex \n120\taudio scenes, we decompose each scene into six complementary textual views: global scene description, speech \n121\ttranscript, speaker identity and style, sound effects, music content, and recording environment. Each view is \n122\tassociated with a dedicated special token (e.g., <|caption|> , <|asr|> , <|speech|> , <|sfx|> , <|music|> , <|env|> ). \n123\tThe general description must be included, while remaining fields are used only when applicable, otherwise filled with \n124\t<|unknown|> . For example, pure speech samples fill <|music|> and <|sfx|> with <|unknown|> . This structured \n125\tconditioning exposes different semantic views to the LLM via explicit special tokens, reducing semantic entanglement \n126\tamong control factors. Audio-Text Alignment Before training the generation model, we first align audio token latents \n127\twith the LLM’s text embedding space. As we show, without explicit alignment, the LLM cannot effectively process the \n128\tcomplex audio scenes necessary for generation. Figure 2 illustrates this alignment process. Following MiDashengLM 7 , \n129\twe perform audio-text alignment via general audio captions. We use the encoder of a custom MiDashengLM-0.6B model as \n130\tour unified semantic-acoustic latent space, which produces continuous representations at 25 Hz. Unlike low-dimensional \n131\tacoustic VAE latents 25 ; 39 , DashengTokenizer preserves both semantic information and acoustic detail, shortening the \n132\tcross-modal mapping from text to audio. During alignment training, an audio adapter maps the DashengTokenizer’s \n133\tlatents into the LLM’s token space. To boost efficiency, we downsample the audio sequence to 5 Hz by grouping k = 5 \n134\tk=5 consecutive encoder frames and projecting them via an MLP: 𝐚 i = MLP ⁡ ( concat ⁡ ( 𝐳 ( i − 1 ) ​ k + \n135\t1 , … , 𝐳 i ​ k ) ) ∈ ℝ d llm , \n136\t\\mathbf{a}_{i}=\\mathrm{MLP}\\left(\\mathrm{concat}(\\mathbf{z}_{(i-1)k+1},\\ldots,\\mathbf{z}_{ik})\\right)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{\\\n137\ttext{llm}}}, (1) where 𝐳 \\mathbf{z} is the audio latent from the encoder of DashengTokenizer and 𝐚 i ∈ ℝ d \n138\tllm \\mathbf{a}_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{\\text{llm}}} is the projected audio token latent at 5 Hz (200 ms per token) for LLM \n139\tinput. Figure 2 : Audio-text alignment in the DashengTokenizer latent space via general caption supervision. \n140\tAutoregressive LLM Input Formulation Given a structured multi-view caption, we construct an input sequence by \n141\tconcatenating the caption text and a sequence of audio token latents: 𝐡 = LLM ⁡ ( [ 𝐞 caption , <|audio_bos|> , \n142\t𝐚 1 , 𝐚 2 , … , 𝐚 N ] ) , \n143\t\\mathbf{h}=\\mathrm{LLM}\\left([\\mathbf{e}_{\\text{caption}},\\texttt{<|audio\\_bos|>},\\mathbf{a}_{1},\\mathbf{a}_{2},\\ldots,\\\n144\tmathbf{a}_{N}]\\right), (2) where 𝐞 caption \\mathbf{e}_{\\text{caption}} is the text embedding of the structured \n145\tcaption, <|audio_bos|> marks the start of audio generation, 𝐚 i \\mathbf{a}_{i} represents the audio latents. The \n146\thidden state 𝐡 i ∈ ℝ d llm \\mathbf{h}_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{\\text{llm}}} at each audio position i i encodes the \n147\tfull context (text semantics, prior audio history, and positional information) and serves as the condition for \n148\tgenerating the i i -th audio token via per-token flow matching ( Section 3.2 ). During training, ground-truth audio \n149\tlatents are fed at each position. 3.2 Per-Token Conditional Flow Matching Unlike full-sequence flow matching that \n150\tgenerates the entire audio latent sequence in one pass we perform conditional flow matching independently for each \n151\taudio token, conditioned on the LLM hidden state 𝐡 i \\mathbf{h}_{i} . This per-token design preserves the \n152\tautoregressive structure’s adaptive length control while retaining the expressiveness of continuous flow-based \n153\tgeneration. Compared to discrete-token autoregressive approaches (e.g., VALL-E 38 ), operating over continuous \n154\thigh-dimensional latents avoids quantization artifacts and supports richer acoustic modeling. Token-Level Generation. \n155\tFor each audio token position i i , the flow-matching DiT generates a group of k = 5 k=5 encoder frames 𝐳 ^ ( i − \n156\t1 ) ​ k + 1 : i ​ k ∈ ℝ k × d a \\hat{\\mathbf{z}}_{(i-1)k+1:ik}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k\\times d_{a}} , by processing \n157\tthe concatenation of latent history frames 𝐳 ^ ( i − 2 ) ​ k + 1 : ( i − 1 ) ​ k \n158\t\\hat{\\mathbf{z}}_{(i-2)k+1:(i-1)k} , and noised target frames, 𝐳 0 ( i ) ∼ 𝒩 ⁡ ( 0 , I ) \n159\t\\mathbf{z}_{0}^{(i)}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I) , conditioned on the timestep embedding and LLM hidden state 𝐡 i ∈ ℝ d \n160\tllm \\mathbf{h}_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{\\text{llm}}} . Each DiT block applies self-attention with rotary position \n161\tembeddings (RoPE) followed by a feed-forward network. Training Objective. Let 𝐳 1 ( i ) ∈ ℝ k × d a \n162\t\\mathbf{z}_{1}^{(i)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k\\times d_{a}} denote the ground-truth frames for token i i , produced by the \n163\tDashengTokenizer encoder at 25 Hz without audio adapter projection. For a random timestep t ∼ 𝒰 ⁡ ( 0 , 1 ) \n164\tt\\sim\\mathcal{U}(0,1) , we construct the interpolated sample 𝐳 t ( i ) = ( 1 − t ) ​ 𝐳 0 ( i ) + t ​ 𝐳 1 \n165\t( i ) \\mathbf{z}_{t}^{(i)}=(1-t)\\mathbf{z}_{0}^{(i)}+t\\,\\mathbf{z}_{1}^{(i)} , where 𝐳 0 ( i ) ∼ 𝒩 ⁡ ( 0 , I \n166\t) \\mathbf{z}_{0}^{(i)}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I) is Gaussian noise. The DiT learns to predict the conditional vector field: \n167\tℒ FM = 𝔼 i , 𝐳 0 ( i ) , 𝐳 1 ( i ) , t ​ [ ‖ v θ ​ ( 𝐳 t ( i ) , t , 𝐡 i , 𝐳 hist ( i ) ) \n168\t− ( 𝐳 1 ( i ) − 𝐳 0 ( i ) ) ‖ 2 2 ] , \n169\t\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{FM}}=\\mathbb{E}_{i,\\mathbf{z}_{0}^{(i)},\\mathbf{z}_{1}^{(i)},t}\\left[\\left\\|v_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{z}_{\n170\tt}^{(i)},t,\\mathbf{h}_{i},\\mathbf{z}_{\\text{hist}}^{(i)})-(\\mathbf{z}_{1}^{(i)}-\\mathbf{z}_{0}^{(i)})\\right\\|_{2}^{2}\\ri\n171\tght], (3) where 𝐡 i \\mathbf{h}_{i} is the LLM hidden state at position i i , 𝐳 hist ( i ) \n172\t\\mathbf{z}_{\\text{hist}}^{(i)} contains the latent history frames, and the loss is masked to only include valid \n173\t(non-padded) audio positions. Inference. At inference, we solve the ODE from noise to signal using a 10-step Euler \n174\tsolver. To improve conditioning strength, we apply classifier-free guidance (CFG) by interpolating between \n175\tunconditional and conditional predictions: v ^ = v θ ​ ( 𝐳 t , t , ∅ , 𝐳 hist ) + w ⋅ ( v θ ​ ( 𝐳 t \n176\t, t , 𝐡 i , 𝐳 hist ) − v θ ​ ( 𝐳 t , t , ∅ , 𝐳 hist ) ) , \n177\t\\hat{v}=v_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\\varnothing,\\mathbf{z}_{\\text{hist}})+w\\cdot\\left(v_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\\mathb\n178\tf{h}_{i},\\mathbf{z}_{\\text{hist}})-v_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{z}_{t},t,\\varnothing,\\mathbf{z}_{\\text{hist}})\\right), (4) where \n179\t∅ \\varnothing is a learnable null embedding and w w is the guidance scale. During training, we randomly replace the \n180\tLLM condition with the null embedding to enable CFG at inference. Generation length is determined by a learned stop \n181\thead that predicts a binary continue/stop distribution over LLM hidden states, trained with balanced cross-entropy \n182\t(hard negatives mined from lowest-confidence non-stop tokens) and combined with ℒ FM \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{FM}} via \n183\tweight λ \\lambda . At inference, generation halts when the stop probability exceeds threshold τ \\tau after a minimum \n184\tof i min i_{\\min} steps. Audio is decoded from the generated latent sequence by the DashengTokenizer decoder: a 5 × \n185\t\\times convolutional upsampler followed by a Vocos 35 vocoder at 16 kHz. 3.3 Implementation Details Model \n186\tConfiguration. We use Qwen3-1.7B 40 as the LLM backbone with full fine-tuning. We extract the encoder from a custom \n187\tMiDashengLM-0.6B model, producing 768-dimensional latents at 25 Hz. The audio projector downsamples to 5 Hz tokens \n188\tprojected to the LLM’s 2048-dimensional space. The DashengTokenizer encoder, audio projector, upsampler, and decoder \n189\tare frozen during the main training phase and the LLM, DiT, and stop head are trained. The DiT in flow matching has 16 \n190\tlayers, hidden dimension 2048, 8 attention heads, and MLP ratio of 4.0. Training and Inference. We train with AdamW \n191\t(8-bit) 6 with the following hyperparameters: batch size 16, learning rate 5 × 10 − 5 5\\times 10^{-5} with cosine \n192\tdecay to 1 × 10 − 6 1\\times 10^{-6} , and gradient clipping at 1.0. Training runs for 800 epochs of 2000 iterations \n193\teach. Audio inputs range from 1 to 20 seconds at 16 kHz. The stop loss weight is λ = 0.01 \\lambda=0.01 , stop \n194\tthreshold τ = 0.5 \\tau=0.5 , and minimum steps i min = 5 i_{\\min}=5 (1 second) at inference. CFG scale is 2.0 with 10 \n195\tODE steps. 4 Experiments 4.1 Experimental Setup Training Data. Our training data comprises two sources. The first is a \n196\tprivate superset of ACAVCaps 30 with 77k hours of audio covering speech, music, and sound effects, derived from \n197\tACAV100M 21 . ACAVCaps uses a multi-expert annotation pipeline that analyzes each audio clip from six domain-specific \n198\tperspectives; we convert these annotations into our structured multi-view caption format. Audio samples are categorized \n199\tinto single-type (S00 = speech-only, 0M0 = music-only, 00A = sound effects) and mixed categories (0MA, S0A, SM0, SMA), \n200\twhere S, M, and A denote the presence of speech, music, and sound effects, respectively, and 0 denotes absence. The \n201\tsecond source consists of dedicated TTS corpora to strengthen speech synthesis: Emilia 12 (Chinese and English \n202\tsubsets), LibriTTS 42 , LJSpeech 17 , AISHELL-3 34 , and WenetSpeech4TTS 26 . For TTS data, we prepend a general \n203\tcaption (“natural human speech”) to align with the multi-view caption format. Table 2 details the language \n204\tdistribution of training data. Table 2 : Language distribution of training data. The ACAVCaps column reports the \n205\tspeech-only (S00) subset; TTS data is predominantly English and Chinese. ACAVCaps (S00) TTS Overall Language Duration \n206\t(h) Proportion (%) Duration (h) Proportion (%) Proportion (%) English 15,368 58.86 11,074 51.90 53.82 Chinese 70 0.27 \n207\t11,949 48.10 24.46 Spanish 2,741 10.50 – – 5.58 Portuguese 1,916 7.34 – – 3.90 Russian 1,217 4.66 – – 2.48 \n208\tFrench 934 3.58 – – 1.90 Japanese 875 3.35 – – 1.78 Korean 848 3.25 – – 1.73 German 842 3.23 – – 1.71 \n209\tOther 1,299 4.98 – – 2.64 Evaluation Benchmarks. For single-type audio and music generation, we report on AudioCaps \n210\t19 and MusicCaps 1 . For speech generation, we evaluate on the Seed-TTS benchmark 2 for English and Chinese \n211\tintelligibility, and adopt the multilingual test set from MiniMax-Speech 43 covering 9 languages. We report CERs \n212\t(Character Error Rate) for Chinese, Japanese, and Korean, and WERs (Word Error Rate) for all other languages. Chinese \n213\tspeech is transcribed using Paraformer-zh 11 , while all other languages are transcribed using Whisper-large-v3 31 . \n214\tFor emotion expressiveness, we use the CV3-Eval Emotional Voice Cloning subset 9 , which retains samples labeled as \n215\thappy, sad, or angry (100 per language), split into text-related and text-unrelated settings depending on whether the \n216\ttarget text is semantically consistent with the target emotion. Emotion classification accuracy is measured using the \n217\temotion2vec model 27 . Our primary benchmark for mixed audio scene generation is MECAT 29 , a held-out test set of \n218\tACAVCaps that contains both single-type and mixed-type audio samples with rich multi-view annotations. Since MECAT is a \n219\tmultilingual benchmark whose speech categories contain utterances in multiple languages, we report objective results on \n220\tthe English subset 5 5 5 The MECAT English subset list is available at \n221\thttps://github.com/xiaomi-research/dasheng-audiogen/tree/main/evaluation . of MECAT for speech-related categories to \n222\tensure fair comparison with baseline methods. Across all benchmarks, we report audio distribution metrics: Fréchet \n223\tAudio Distance (FAD) 18 , Fréchet Distance (FD), and Kullback–Leibler divergence (KL) 24 , text similarity metrics: \n224\tContrastive Language-Audio Pretraining (CLAP) 10 , and speech-related metrics: WER/CER. In all tables, bold and \n225\tunderlined denote the best and second-best results, respectively. Table 3 : Results on AudioCaps and MusicCaps. \n226\tAudioCaps MusicCaps Method FAD VGG \\mathrm{FAD}_{\\mathrm{VGG}} ↓ \\downarrow FD PANNS \\mathrm{FD}_{\\mathrm{PANNS}} ↓ \n227\t\\downarrow KL ↓ \\downarrow CLAP ↑ \\uparrow FAD VGG \\mathrm{FAD}_{\\mathrm{VGG}} ↓ \\downarrow FD PANNS \n228\t\\mathrm{FD}_{\\mathrm{PANNS}} ↓ \\downarrow KL ↓ \\downarrow CLAP ↑ \\uparrow TangoFlux 2.26 19.13 1.19 0.58 - - - - \n229\tMusicGen - - - - 3.80 - 1.31 0.28 UniFlow-Audio 5.74 17.18 1.43 0.48 4.05 27.12 1.87 0.24 Dasheng AudioGen 3.19 26.06 \n230\t1.86 0.44 1.37 18.45 1.37 0.33 MiDashengLM-Gen 5.01 26.13 1.91 0.36 2.28 14.58 1.24 0.33 Table 4 : Speech \n231\tintelligibility on the Seed-TTS benchmark. Method EN WER(%) ↓ \\downarrow ZH CER(%) ↓ \\downarrow Seed-TTS 2.25 1.12 \n232\tQwen3-TTS 1.24 0.77 Dasheng AudioGen 12.15 > > 100 MiDashengLM-Gen 2.79 3.87 Table 5 : Multilingual TTS evaluation \n233\t(WER/CER% ↓ \\downarrow ). Language MiniMax ElevenLabs Dasheng AudioGen MiDashengLM-Gen English 2.16 2.34 8.60 2.42 \n234\tChinese 2.25 16.03 99.62 3.51 Spanish 1.03 1.08 9.31 3.11 Portuguese 1.88 1.33 8.98 8.04 Russian 4.28 3.88 36.34 13.19 \n235\tFrench 4.10 5.22 17.96 12.53 Japanese 3.52 10.65 109.21 16.45 Korean 1.75 1.87 100.25 5.49 German 1.91 0.57 24.15 4.38 \n236\tTable 6 : Emotion expressiveness on CV3-Eval (accuracy ↑ \\uparrow ). Text-Related Text-Unrelated Method Happy Sad \n237\tAngry Happy Sad Angry F5-TTS 0.92 0.52 0.72 0.80 0.28 0.64 CosyVoice3-1.5B 0.86 0.64 0.72 0.64 0.44 0.48 CosyVoice3 \n238\tDiffRO-EMO {}_{\\text{DiffRO-EMO}} 0.98 0.68 0.84 0.98 0.50 0.68 MiDashengLM-Gen 0.98 0.96 0.92 0.78 0.52 0.62 4.2 Sound \n239\tEffect and Music Generation To validate our approach’s capabilities on single-type audio generation, we report \n240\tresults on AudioCaps and MusicCaps in Table 3 . We compare against TangoFlux 16 , MusicGen 5 , UniFlow-Audio 39 , and \n241\tDasheng AudioGen 28 . On AudioCaps, our model trails the task-optimized TangoFlux (FAD 2.26 vs. 5.01). This gap is \n242\texpected: DashengLM-Gen is trained on mixed-audio scenes and TTS corpora, not on pure sound-effect data, and its \n243\tarchitecture prioritizes cross-modal alignment and multi-source coordination over single-source acoustic fidelity. \n244\tTangoFlux, by contrast, is a dedicated text-to-audio model trained specifically on sound-effect corpora and uses a \n245\tnon-autoregressive flow-matching architecture that can optimize acoustic quality globally in a single pass. On \n246\tMusicCaps, our approach achieves notably better FD (14.58 vs. 18.45) and KL (1.24 vs. 1.37) compared to Dasheng \n247\tAudioGen, demonstrating that the LLM backbone provides stronger cross-modal alignment for music generation through \n248\tcausal attention over longer contexts. 4.3 Speech Generation Speech Generation on Seed-TTS. Table 4 reports WER on the \n249\tSeed-TTS benchmark 2 , which evaluates speech intelligibility on English and Chinese test sets. Our approach achieves \n250\t2.79% on Seed-EN and 3.87% on Seed-ZH, substantially improving over Dasheng AudioGen (12.15% and > > 100%, \n251\trespectively). This demonstrates that the LLM backbone leverages its pre-trained language understanding to produce \n252\tintelligible speech. A 2.2 × \\times gap remains against specialized TTS systems such as Qwen3-TTS (1.24%/0.77%) and \n253\tSeed-TTS (2.25%/1.12%), primarily because our model is trained on mixed-audio scenes rather than optimized specifically \n254\tfor speech intelligibility. Multilingual Speech Generation. To evaluate multilingual capability, Table 5 reports \n255\tWER/CER across 9 languages. Our model achieves competitive WER in high-resource languages (2.42% English, 3.51% \n256\tChinese), where training data is abundant. For under-represented languages such as Japanese (16.45%) and Russian \n257\t(13.19%), the higher WER is consistent with the training data distribution shown in Table 2 , where these languages \n258\thave limited representation in the TTS corpus. Compared to Dasheng AudioGen, which suffers from extremely high error \n259\trates on most languages (e.g., 99.62% Chinese, 109.21% Japanese), our LLM-based approach demonstrates dramatically \n260\tbetter multilingual speech intelligibility. Emotion Expressiveness. To evaluate emotion control capability, we conduct \n261\texperiments on CV3-Eval, which measures emotion classification accuracy under two settings: text-related (emotion cue \n262\tpresent in the text prompt) and text-unrelated (emotion conveyed purely through prosody without textual cues). Table 6 \n263\treports results for three emotions (happy, sad, angry). Our approach achieves the highest text-related emotion accuracy \n264\tacross all three emotions (0.98/0.96/0.92), demonstrating that the LLM backbone effectively conditions on emotional \n265\tcues in the text prompt to generate appropriately expressive speech. In text-unrelated scenarios, where emotion must be \n266\tconveyed solely through prosody, CosyVoice3 DiffRO-EMO {}_{\\text{DiffRO-EMO}} achieves the strongest performance \n267\t(0.98/0.50/0.68), suggesting that its specialized emotion control mechanism better preserves prosodic emotion without \n268\ttextual cues. Our model achieves the best sad accuracy (0.52) in this setting, indicating competitive emotion \n269\texpressiveness even without explicit textual guidance. 4.4 MECAT Benchmark To comprehensively evaluate model \n270\tperformance in complex mixed-audio scenes, we conduct experiments on the MECAT benchmark. We compare against TangoFlux \n271\t16 , MusicGen 5 , Qwen3-TTS 14 , and Dasheng AudioGen 28 . Single-Type Categories. Table 7 compares models on MECAT \n272\tsingle-type categories. On the sound effects category (00A), Dasheng AudioGen achieves better FAD (4.25 vs. 6.05), \n273\twhile our approach achieves better FD (18.74 vs. 19.37) and KL (1.32 vs. 1.36). On the music category (0M0), our \n274\tapproach demonstrates a significant advantage in FD (6.82 vs. 14.90), reducing the distance by more than 2 × \\times , \n275\twhile maintaining competitive FAD (1.97 vs. 1.66) and KL (0.57 vs. 0.61). On the speech category (S00), our approach \n276\tachieves substantially better FAD (0.87 vs. 1.76) and CLAP (0.35 vs. 0.34), indicating stronger alignment between \n277\tgenerated speech and its acoustic context. The LLM backbone enables better modeling of speech together with \n278\tenvironmental details, whereas Dasheng AudioGen generates speech more detached from the scene. Table 7 : Results on \n279\tMECAT single-type categories. Category Method FAD VGG \\mathrm{FAD}_{\\mathrm{VGG}} ↓ \\downarrow FD PANNS \n280\t\\mathrm{FD}_{\\mathrm{PANNS}} ↓ \\downarrow KL ↓ \\downarrow CLAP ↑ \\uparrow 00A TangoFlux 15.68 48.81 2.30 0.30 \n281\tMusicGen 43.68 87.41 5.99 -0.03 Qwen3-TTS 51.42 153.55 5.90 -0.04 Dasheng AudioGen 4.25 19.37 1.36 0.37 MiDashengLM-Gen \n282\t6.05 18.74 1.32 0.35 0M0 TangoFlux 5.04 24.97 0.82 0.32 MusicGen 4.53 19.75 0.88 0.26 Qwen3-TTS 21.68 137.10 4.99 -0.03 \n283\tDasheng AudioGen 1.66 14.90 0.61 0.27 MiDashengLM-Gen 1.97 6.82 0.57 0.28 S00 TangoFlux 10.54 53.15 1.63 0.29 MusicGen \n284\t26.74 89.71 5.38 0.04 Qwen3-TTS 8.75 27.55 0.84 0.31 Dasheng AudioGen 1.76 3.93 0.40 0.34 MiDashengLM-Gen 0.87 4.15 \n285\t0.44 0.35 Mixed Categories. Table 8 reports results on the mixed-audio categories. Across all speech-containing mixed \n286\tcategories (S0A, SM0, SMA), our approach consistently achieves lower FAD than Dasheng AudioGen: 1.54 vs. 1.75 on S0A, \n287\t0.98 vs. 1.70 on SM0, and 1.88 vs. 2.17 on SMA. However, Dasheng AudioGen retains advantages on feature-level \n288\tdistribution metrics (FD and KL) across these categories, suggesting its non-autoregressive architecture better \n289\tcaptures fine-grained acoustic details. The overall tradeoff indicates that the LLM backbone improves multi-source \n290\tcoordination and spectral quality, while the non-autoregressive DiT has an edge in distributional fidelity for isolated \n291\taspects of the audio. On the non-speech mixed category (0MA), Dasheng AudioGen achieves better FAD (3.86 vs. 4.23), \n292\twhile our approach achieves better FD (28.32 vs. 31.13) and KL (1.25 vs. 1.36). Table 8 : Results on MECAT mixed-audio \n293\tcategories. Category Method FAD VGG \\mathrm{FAD}_{\\mathrm{VGG}} ↓ \\downarrow FD PANNS \\mathrm{FD}_{\\mathrm{PANNS}} \n294\t↓ \\downarrow KL ↓ \\downarrow CLAP ↑ \\uparrow 0MA TangoFlux 9.71 58.44 2.45 0.32 MusicGen 13.06 60.98 2.86 0.19 \n295\tQwen3-TTS 23.17 149.24 5.28 -0.03 Dasheng AudioGen 3.86 31.13 1.36 0.31 MiDashengLM-Gen 4.23 28.32 1.25 0.32 S0A \n296\tTangoFlux 10.92 43.94 2.23 0.36 MusicGen 30.38 89.09 5.60 0.00 Qwen3-TTS 19.62 62.84 2.42 0.15 Dasheng AudioGen 1.75 \n297\t8.56 0.69 0.36 MiDashengLM-Gen 1.54 8.84 0.72 0.36 SM0 TangoFlux 11.57 40.77 1.11 0.34 MusicGen 19.83 57.28 2.38 0.14 \n298\tQwen3-TTS 10.20 63.15 2.16 0.19 Dasheng AudioGen 1.70 6.69 0.34 0.33 MiDashengLM-Gen 0.98 6.78 0.40 0.34 SMA TangoFlux \n299\t11.40 50.58 2.05 0.29 MusicGen 20.18 69.19 3.41 0.11 Qwen3-TTS 16.53 83.01 2.45 0.10 Dasheng AudioGen 2.17 17.75 0.63 \n300\t0.38 MiDashengLM-Gen 1.88 20.60 0.85 0.35 4.5 Ablation Experiments We ablate two core designs of MiDashengLM-Gen: the \n301\taudio-text alignment stage and the DiT architecture for the flow matching decoder. Importance of Audio-Text Alignment. \n302\tTo validate the necessity of the audio-text alignment stage (Section 3.1 ), we compare our full model against a variant \n303\tthat skips alignment entirely: the audio adapter (projection layer) is randomly initialized and the LLM retains its \n304\toriginal Qwen3 pre-trained weights without alignment training, while the DashengTokenizer encoder keeps its pre-trained \n305\tweights. The generation training procedure remains identical in both settings. Table 9 shows that removing audio-text \n306\talignment degrades performance across all benchmarks. FAD increases by 47–88% on audio generation tasks (e.g., 5.01 \n307\t→ \\to 9.39 on AudioCaps), indicating weakened audio quality and text relevance. The impact on speech generation is \n308\tparticularly severe: Seed-TTS English WER rises from 2.79% to 12.17% (4.4 × \\times ), Chinese CER from 3.87% to 14.17% \n309\t(3.7 × \\times ), and mean multilingual WER from 7.68% to 31.73% (4.1 × \\times ). This disproportionate degradation in \n310\tspeech intelligibility suggests that audio-text alignment is especially critical for speech generation, where the LLM \n311\tmust map text semantics precisely to phonetic content. Without a pre-aligned embedding space, the generation stage \n312\tcannot effectively leverage the LLM’s language understanding for intelligible speech synthesis. Table 9 : Ablation on \n313\taudio-text alignment. “w/o Align” uses randomly initialized audio adapter and original Qwen3 LLM weights without \n314\talignment pre-training. MECAT Single/Mixed report weighted averages across their respective categories; Multilingual \n315\treports the mean across 9 languages. Benchmark Metric w/o Align MiDashengLM-Gen AudioCaps FAD ↓ \\downarrow 9.39 5.01 \n316\tCLAP ↑ \\uparrow 0.28 0.36 MusicCaps FAD ↓ \\downarrow 4.06 2.28 CLAP ↑ \\uparrow 0.30 0.33 MECAT Single FAD ↓ \n317\t\\downarrow 3.18 1.86 CLAP ↑ \\uparrow 0.32 0.33 MECAT Mixed FAD ↓ \\downarrow 2.46 1.35 CLAP ↑ \\uparrow 0.32 0.35 \n318\tSeed-TTS EN WER(%) ↓ \\downarrow 12.17 2.79 ZH CER(%) ↓ \\downarrow 14.17 3.87 Multilingual Mean WER(%) ↓ \n319\t\\downarrow 31.73 7.68 Scaling DiT Width to Match Audio Latent Dimensionality. Recent work on diffusion transformers has \n320\tshown that the model width must match the token dimensionality of the latent space, because noise injection in \n321\tdiffusion training expands the data manifold to a full-rank distribution 44 . We investigate whether this principle \n322\textends to audio generation with MiDashengLM-Gen. To this end, we conduct single-sample overfit experiments with \n323\tvarying DiT width and depth configurations under a fixed parameter budget. Figure 3 shows the training loss and STFT L2 \n324\tdistance curves for our final model configuration (768-dim audio latent + Qwen3-1.7B), with the DashengTokenizer \n325\tencode-to-decode reconstruction as the topline. Table 10 further validates the convergence pattern across three \n326\tadditional model scales, with the DiT total parameters fixed across all configurations by trading off width and depth. \n327\tFigure 3 : Training loss and STFT L2 distance curves for different DiT width/depth configurations. Topline denotes \n328\tDashengTokenizer encode-to-decode reconstruction. Table 10 : Convergence analysis of DiT width across different model \n329\tscales. ✓ denotes successful convergence. Setting DiT Width Train Loss ↓ \\downarrow Convergence 768-dim + Qwen3 \n330\t0.6B 752 0.0422 × \\times 768 0.0343 × \\times 784 0.0171 ✓ 1024 0.0110 ✓ 1280-dim + Qwen3 0.6B 1248 0.0354 × \n331\t\\times 1280 0.0144 ✓ 1312 0.0084 ✓ 1536 0.0064 ✓ 1280-dim + Qwen3 4B 1248 0.0365 × \\times 1280 0.0126 ✓ 1312 \n332\t0.0081 ✓ 1536 0.0063 ✓ Figure 3 reveals that the DiT width must strictly exceed the audio latent dimensionality for \n333\tsuccessful convergence. When width falls below or equals this threshold (768 for 768-dim audio), the model fails to \n334\tconverge ( × \\times ) with an STFT L2 distance of 3.89, well above the topline of 1.30. Only once width exceeds the \n335\tlatent dimensionality does training converge successfully ( ✓ ). Table 10 confirms that this pattern generalizes \n336\tacross different LLM scales (0.6B and 4B) and audio latent dimensionalities (768 and 1280), mirroring the theoretical \n337\tfinding by Zheng et al. 44 in image diffusion. 5 Discussion and Conclusion We presented MiDashengLM-Gen, an \n338\tautoregressive approach that combines a pre-trained LLM backbone with per-token flow matching for variable-length \n339\tmixed-audio scene generation. The key insight is that high-dimensional semantic-acoustic latents are viable for \n340\tLLM-conditioned generation when the decoder architecture is properly scaled — specifically, the DiT width must exceed \n341\tthe audio latent dimensionality for convergence. Comprehensive evaluations demonstrate that MiDashengLM-Gen \n342\tsubstantially improves speech intelligibility over prior unified models (2.79% vs. 12.15% 28 on Seed-TTS English), \n343\tapproaching dedicated TTS systems (1.24%), while maintaining competitive mixed-audio scene generation and supporting \n344\tmultilingual output. Ablation studies reveal two critical design requirements: audio-text alignment pre-training is \n345\tessential for bridging the modality gap (removing it degrades speech WER by 4 × 4\\times ), and the DiT decoder width \n346\tmust strictly exceed the audio latent dimensionality, consistent with theoretical findings in image diffusion 44 . \n347\tMiDashengLM-Gen has several limitations. Variable-length generation is bounded by the training data distribution \n348\t(1–20 seconds); generating coherent audio beyond this range remains open. Speech intelligibility still trails \n349\tdedicated TTS systems by a factor of 2.2 × \\times on English, with larger gaps for low-resource languages where TTS \n350\ttraining data is scarce. The model supports only coarse speaker-style control, without voice cloning or explicit \n351\tspeaker identity conditioning. Future work will explore scaling to longer durations, improved low-resource language \n352\tperformance, voice cloning, and finer-grained controllability such as audio editing and explicit temporal control. 6 \n353\tAcknowledgement This work makes use of the ACAVCaps 30 , Emilia 12 , LibriTTS 42 , LJSpeech 17 , AISHELL-3 34 , and \n354\tWenetSpeech4TTS 26 datasets for training. For evaluation, we use the AudioCaps 19 , MusicCaps 1 , Seed-TTS 2 , \n355\tMiniMax-Speech 43 , CV3-Eval 9 , and MECAT 29 benchmarks. The authors confirm that the use of the ACAVCaps, Emilia, \n356\tAISHELL-3, and WenetSpeech4TTS datasets is strictly limited to academic research purposes and does not involve any \n357\tcommercial activities. All datasets are used in compliance with their respective licensing agreements and original \n358\tcitations. References [1] A. Agostinelli, T. I. Denk, Z. Borsos, J. Engel, M. Verzetti, A. Caillon, Q. Huang, A. \n359\tJansen, A. Roberts, M. Tagliasacchi, M. Sharifi, N. Zeghidour, and C. Frank (2023) MusicLM: generating music from text \n360\t. In International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML Workshop) , Cited by: §1 , §2.1 , §4.1 , §6 . [2] P. \n361\tAnastassiou, J. Chung, A. Deepak, S. E. Florez, Y. He, S. Jiang, T. Kaftan, J. Li, F. Lin, Y. Luo, et al. (2024) \n362\tSeed-tts: a family of high-quality versatile speech generation models . arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.02430 . Cited by: §1 \n363\t, §2.1 , §4.1 , §4.3 , §6 . [3] Z. Borsos, M. Sharifi, D. Vincent, E. Kharitonov, N. Zeghidour, and M. Tagliasacchi \n364\t(2